{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/hunter-the-vigil/365937-convention-the-firetouched-movement","id":365937,"title":"[Convention] The Firetouched Movement","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Hunter: The Vigil"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-01-10T20:16:52-08:00","id":365937,"title":"[Convention] The Firetouched Movement","content":"<i>&quot;Are you ready?&quot;<br /> <br /> Tim looked up at the woman; funny, she looked a bit like his high-school History teacher. Well, other than the branding iron she was holding in her hand. At his side, Brad was looking a bit uncomfortable; considering what was coming next, it was understandable.<br /> <br /> &quot;Tim, you don't have to do this; we can find someone else -&quot;<br /> &quot;After that furry bastard broke both my legs? I'll be lucky if I can walk again, let alone go hunting. No, you take it.&quot;<br /> &quot;But -&quot;<br /> &quot;I've made my decision. Can we just get this over with?&quot;<br /> <br /> The moment the brand touched his skin, Tim screamed, and felt the strength leave his limbs.<br /> Moments later, Brad joined in, but strength flowed into his.</i><br /> <div align=\"center\"><span style=\"font-size:16px\"><b>The Firetouched Movement</b></span></div> <br /> <br /> The Movement is relatively new as hunting movements come... though it's particular methods span the centuries. If an outsider (or an initiate) asks a member of the movement where their particular practice of ceremonial branding comes from, they get a story about a Middle-Eastern book unearthed in the early 19th century listing methods of transferring strengths from one person to another, along with a suitable background of experimentation, research, and self-sacrifice. Oh, the details (like dates, or where the book was found) change between tellings, but that's the essential <i>story</i> that gets passed along and down from master to apprentice.<br /> <br /> Of course, it's complete bunk, but it's a good story nonetheless. It's better than the truth - that no one knows <i>where</i> the practice arose, or how it exactly works. That it seems to crop up in the most unlikely places - from the suburbs of Boston to remote villages in Bangladesh. Or that those suitably occult symbols they burn onto donors and recipients alike <i>don't mean anything</i>... except to the practitioners, for whom the hot brands whisper and screech, even in their dreams. <i>Especially</i> in their dreams.<br /> <br /> The Movement is a motley community of enablers, who communicate with each-other every so often with friendly emails and letters, maybe a phone-call or two. Because while some people practice the Vigil by candle-light, they practice it by the light of glowing iron.<br /> <br /> <b>The Enemy</b><br /> The Movement's dispersed members rarely hunt anything <i>directly</i>; they anchor themselves to a cell, and offer their services. To most Hunters, especially those without the support system of being part of a compact or conspiracy, the prospect of someone that can give you that edge against that <i>thing</i> that just painted Steve all over the wall is intoxicating.<br /> But when all it said and done, the Firetouched are drawn to certain prey, and away from others - while in most cases this is individual, they do have a distinct preference for keeping prey alive. Monsters make <i>excellent</i> donors.<br /> <br /> <b>Hunters</b><br /> You and your buds started hunting monsters about a year ago; lately, though, you've been dreaming of a symbol. You know what it means, just not in a way you could explain. And scratching it in doesn't do anything - maybe if you <i>burned</i> it in, it'd stick...<br /> <br /> You made your living as a cowherd for a few years, and then you fucked up; you tripped after branding one of the cattle and stuck yourself in the leg. The damn thing got sick and died a few weeks later, but you felt <i>damn</i> good the whole time - the brand didn't even hurt. Maybe it wasn't a fluke. Maybe you should try to see if you can repeat the experience.<br /> <br /> You were a blood-slave to some vampire-thing for who knows how long until you were rescued by a group of strange men with burns on their chests. They asked you to join, and, well, you never were too good at making decisions. Now you have the vampire-thing locked in a basement, with the promise that your new powers will last as long as it stays alive.<br /> <br /> <b>Attitudes</b><br /> Imagine if you could brand the three most intelligent scientists and become the smartest person alive? Wouldn't that be amazing, well the <b>Progressives</b> say it's not. What they would consider amazing is using that intelligence to solve global warming, or cure cancer. Progressives have a practical goal, and with the right brands they might achieve it.<br /> <br /> Most Firetouched are <b>Addicts</b> they want to be stronger, they want to fly, they want to be sexually irresistible, they want more. Always more. An Addict usually doesn't start shaking if they go too long without a brand and can easily pass up any number of oppotunities for brands if it's not the one they're looking for, but to an Addict gaining more brands has become a reason all onto itself.<br /> <br /> Finally you have the <b>Transhumans</b>. A group of mystics who consider themselves incomplete on a spiritual level and believe the right brand can fill in the missing spots. Perhaps if they branded themselves with a way to subsists on water alone they would be free to live a life of prayer or maybe if they gained the ability to see magnetic fields they could commune with Earth Mother Gaia. Transhumans are strange people on a pilgrimage to become something more than human.<br /> <br /> <b>Status</b><br /> As the Firetouched Movement doesn't have a formal hierarchy, it also doesn't have any real &quot;status ladder&quot; that people can climb. However, respected members of the loose-knit community tend to be able to draw on more resources than some of their more reclusive peers.<br /> \u2022 : The Brander is inducted into the Movement and is taught the rudiments of branding, allowing them to purchase the Fire's Touch merit.<br /> \u2022\u2022\u2022 : The Brander is respected within the wide-reaching network, giving them three Contacts with organizations that have come in contact with members of the Movement.<br /> \u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022 : The Hunter is considered to be an expert in their particular &quot;field&quot;; other members are happy to trade influence for information, giving the Hunter four dots of Allies, split amongst organizations that have come in contact with members of the Movement - however, they cannot put more than two of these dots in any given organization.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\"><b>Endowment: Fire's Touch (\u2022 - \u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022)</b></span><br /> The Movement's endowment is not the brands they give out like candy; instead, it's the fact that they <i>can</i> perform such brandings. This merit gives three benefits:<br /> <br /> 1) Each dot gives the Hunter the knowledge necessary to administer one type of Brand.<br /> 2) Each dot also gives the Hunter a free Speciality related to creation; Crafts and Expression are the most common, though other skills are possible.<br /> 3) As a peripheral benefit (which is rarely noticed), the Hunter adds their Fire's Touch dot rating to their Resolve + Composure when resisting Disquiet.<br /> <br /> <b>Brands</b><br /> Brands are deceptively simple - from the outside, it looks like you just need a length of wire in the right shape and an open flame. Heat up the metal, brand the donor, brand the recipient, and you're done! Of course, half the process involves knowing how to shape the metal the right way, and another 2/5ths is knowing <i>who</i> to Brand.<br /> <br /> The donor <i>has</i> to have at least three dots in the trait the Brand is designed to transfer, and cannot already be a donor for that type of Brand; you only get to give up your gifts so many times. And then there's the fact that the recipient can't benefit from more than three of the same type of brand at once - or the fact that a donor dying means the recipient loses the benefits of the brand, while a recipient dying means that the donor loses the drawbacks.<br /> <br /> But other than that, the brander just twists up a piece of metal, brands the donor on the chest (which deals 1L), and then brands the recipient (who takes another 1L), who receives the benefits. The recipient must spend 1 XP before the benefits appear; they may immediately spend this XP, however, and may enter into XP &quot;debt&quot;, where any XP gained is immediately directed to pay for the Brand.<br /> <br /> There are two types of Brands:<br /> <br /> <i>Attribute Brands:</i> A Brand exists for each Attribute; the recipient gains an extra dot of that attribute, which does not count towards their cap; if the donor's attribute was at least 4, the recipient also increases the number of dice spending Willpower on a dicepool involving that Attribute gives them by 1. However, as long as the recipient lives, the donor's corresponding attribute is reduced to one, and their maximum for that Attribute is reduced by 3.<br /> <br /> <i>Dread Brands:</i> A Brand exists for each Dread Power; the recipient gains a dot of the donors particular version of that Dread Power, to a maximum of 5; if the donor had at least 4 dots in the Dread Power, the recipient gains two dots instead (still to a maximum of 5). However, as long as the recipient lives, the donor's dot rating in that Dread Power is reduced to one, and their maximum is reduced to two dots.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I have decided, after some thought, that I don't really feel happy on these forums. I might decide to come back to post. Who knows - but right 